                "text": "**Identifying Corner Logic**\n\nI'm focusing on how the grid coordinates define corner points. It appears the first coordinate pair indicates the top-left and the second the top-right, establishing the grid's width. I'm now exploring how this translates to the patterned output.\n\n**Observing Value Placement**\n\nMy analysis of the provided data reveals that the '1' values form nested square-like structures. I'm now investigating why certain rows, specifically rows 6 and 8 in the sample, exhibit a full '1' fill across the initial columns. This pattern seems inconsistent with the L-shape hypothesis and suggests a different generation rule. I am also examining the '2' values in the third example to see if they follow a similar positional logic.\n\n**Exploring L-shape Expansion**\n\nI'm re-evaluating the concentric L-shapes. It seems their expansion proceeds in even steps, and when a shell reaches the grid's mid-row, it might transition from horizontal to vertical extension. I'm investigating how this impacts the '2' and '4' values in Example 3.\n\n**Tracing Vertical Lines**\n\nI've identified that at columns 6, 8, 10, and 12, rows 0 through 5 are consistently filled with '2'. This suggests vertical lines extending to the right edge. For the '4' values, I see L-shells expanding, with row 6 acting as a midpoint, causing the expansion to continue horizontally at specific columns.\n\n**Re-evaluating Example 1**\n\nI'm revisiting Example 1 and the placement of '1' and '2'. It appears '1' expands outwards from (0,0) in L-shells, but when it reaches column 4 (the midpoint), it transitions into horizontal stripes at rows 6 and 8. Similarly, '2' expands from (0,9) in L-shells from the top-right.\n\n**Testing Chebyshev Distance**\n\nI am now exploring the hypothesis that cells are colored based on the minimum Chebyshev distance to the specified corner points. If this minimum distance is even and unique to a single corner, the cell takes that corner's color; otherwise, it becomes 0. This seems to align with Example 2 and 4.\n\n",
